\nL113: You signed in with another tab or window. Reload to refresh your session. You signed out in another tab or window. Reload to refresh your session. You switched accounts on another tab or window. Reload to refresh your session. Dismiss alert\nL114: \nL115:   1. cite61†databricks L116:   2. cite62†cli L117: ## Repository navigation\nL118: \nL119:   *   * cite62†Code L120:   * cite63†Issues93 (93) L121:   * cite64†Pull requests234 (234) L122:   * cite65†Actions L123:   * cite66†Projects L124:   * cite67†Security and quality L125:   * cite68†Insights L126: \nL127: More items\nL128: \nL129: # v1.20.0\nL130: \nL131: cite69†Latest L132: \nL133: Compare\nL134: \nL135: # Choose a tag to compare\nL136: \nL137: ## Sorry, something went wrong.\nL138: \nL139: Filter\nL140: \nL141: Loading [Input]\nL142: \nL143: ## Sorry, something went wrong.\nL144: \nL145: ### Uh oh!\nL146: \nL147: There was an error while loading. Please reload this page.\nL148: ## No results found\nL149: \nL150: cite70†View all tags L151: \nL152: cite71†Image: @oss-cli†avatars.githubusercontent.com cite72†oss-cli released this 07 Oct 12:16\nL153: \nL154: Immutable release. Only release title and notes can be modified.\nL155: \nL156: cite73†v1.20.0 L157: \nL158: cite74†`5ed9313` L159: \nL160: This commit was created on GitHub.com and signed with GitHub’s verified signature.\nL161: \nL162: GPG key ID: B5690EEEBB952194\nL163: \nL164: Verified\nL165: \nL166: cite75†Learn about vigilant mode†docs.github.com .\nL167: \nL168: ## Release v1.20.0\nL169: ### Notable Changes\nL170: \nL171:   * Remove the Terraform deployment engine. `bundle.engine: terraform` and `DATABRICKS_BUNDLE_ENGINE=terraform` now error, and a failed migration of existing Terraform state is reported as an error instead of falling back to Terraform. To keep deploying with Terraform, use Databricks CLI v1.19.x. (cite76†#6888 , cite77†#6889 )\nL172: ### CLI\nL173:   * `databricks aitools install` now supports Kiro, installing Databricks agent skills into its skills directory. (cite78†#6908 )\nL174:   * Fixed `databricks api` corrupting integers larger than 2^53 (such as job and pipeline ids) — request bodies and responses now preserve them exactly. (cite79†#6884 )\nL175:   * Added `--auth-mode` and `--set <plugin>.<resourceKey>.authMode=obo|sp|both` to `databricks apps init` so AppKit resources can be accessed on behalf of the user, by the service principal, or both. The default stays service principal. (cite80†#6886 )\nL176:   * `databricks apps init` now requires a value for every field a service principal resource binding references, prompting for missing values in an interactive terminal and otherwise failing with the `--set` key to use, instead of creating a project with unset variables. (cite81†#6903 )\nL177:   * Add `databricks apps init --package-manager <npm|pnpm>` to select the package manager for Node.js templates. Infer the default quietly from template lockfiles and AppKit version, check prerequisites before creating files, and preserve template formatting and pnpm version pins. (cite82†#6902 )\nL178:   * Select npm or pnpm from `packageManager` declarations and lockfiles for `apps validate` and project validation during `apps deploy`. (cite83†#6892 )\nL179:   * Fix `auth docker host` reporting the credential helper as configured when its executable is missing from `PATH`. (cite84†#6880 )\nL180:   * Warn when the CLI binary was built more than 6 months ago and recommend updating. (cite85†#6898 )\nL181: ### AI Runtime\nL182: \nL183:   * Add an experimental rank-partitioned container images to AI Runtime jobs. (cite86†#6841 )\nL184:   * Support snapshot fields directly under `code_source` without requiring `type` or a nested `snapshot` block. (cite87†#6927 )\nL185:   * Map AIR priority and Unity Catalog image fields when converting run configurations to bundles. (cite88†#6905 )\nL186:   * Add workspace backend validation to `air run --dry-run`. (cite89†#6934 )\nL187: ### Bundles\nL188:   * Warn that `bundle.terraform` is deprecated and has no effect since the Terraform deployment engine was removed. (cite90†#6940 )\nL189:   * Direct engine now detects and applies an explicitly configured zero-value boolean or float (e.g. `gcp_attributes.use_preemptible_executors: false`, `azure_attributes.spot_bid_max_price: 0`) added to a resource first deployed without the field, matching the existing handling of an explicit integer zero. (cite91†#6882 )\nL190:   * Fix `bundle deployment migrate` failing with \"no such file or directory\" when the Terraform state has no resources or the configuration no longer declares any of them. (cite92†#6958 )\nL191:   * `bundle run` and `pipelines run` now send the per-update `development` parameter for pipelines in development mode targets. Setting `development` on a pipeline is deprecated and now emits a warning; use `mode: development` instead. (cite93†#6863 )\nL192:   * Remove the hidden `bundle debug terraform` command. (cite94†#6933 )\nL193:   * Add support for `run_as.group_name` at the bundle and target levels for jobs and pipelines. (cite95†#6676 )\nL194:   * Fix recreating a secret scope that was deleted outside of the bundle with the direct deployment engine. (cite96†#6970 )\nL195:   * Accept title-case booleans (`True`/`False`, as rendered by Azure Pipelines) for boolean variables, and accept the same boolean strings (`yes`/`no`, `on`/`off`, ...) in Python bundles as in YAML. (cite97†#6942 )\nL196: ### Dependency Updates\nL197: \nL198:   * Bump `github.com/databricks/databricks-sdk-go` from v0.182.0 to v0.185.0. (cite98†#6928 )\nL199: \nL200: Assets 17\nL201: \nL202: Loading\nL203: \nL204: ### Uh oh!\nL205: \nL206: There was an error while loading. Please reload this page.\nL207: \nL208: You can’t perform that action at this time.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDatabricks SQL alerts | Databricks on AWS (https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/sql/user/alerts/)\nciteturn48view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/sql/user/alerts/\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 49\nL0: cite0†iframe†www.googletagmanager.com L1: \nL2: cite1†Skip to main content L3: \nL4: [Button: On this page]\nL5: \nL6: Last updated on Oct 6, 2026\nL7: # Databricks SQL alerts\nL8: \nL9: Databricks SQL alerts run queries on a schedule and notify you when a condition that you define is met against the query result. When you schedule an alert, its associated query runs and the condition is evaluated. You can also view an alert history to review the results of past evaluations.\nL10: \nL11: To learn how to work with legacy alerts instead, see cite2†What are legacy alerts? .\nL12: ## What you can do with alertscite3†​ L13: \nL14: Alerts let you monitor any SQL query result on a schedule. Use them to track business KPIs, monitor data quality, watch cost trends, and catch operational issues across your Databricks workloads. Common patterns across Databricks include:\nL15:   * Monitor business metrics in metric views: Reference a Unity Catalog metric view by its fully qualified name in the alert query to monitor governed business metrics. See cite4†Alert on metric views .\nL16:   * Detect data quality issues and anomalies: Pair alerts with Unity Catalog data quality monitors and anomaly detection so an unexpected metric, distribution shift, or profile change sends a notification. See cite5†Alerts for anomaly detection and cite6†Profile alerts .\nL17:   * Track usage and cost: Build alerts on system tables for serverless billing or ingestion to catch unexpected spend. See cite7†Monitor the cost of serverless compute and cite8†Monitor managed ingestion pipeline cost .\nL18:   * Watch SQL warehouse and query health: Alert on warehouse events or query history to catch slow queries, failed sessions, or capacity issues. See cite9†Example queries for monitoring SQL warehouse activity and cite10†Warehouse events system table reference .\nL19:   * Audit access and security events: Alert on audit log queries to flag unusual workspace activity. See cite11†Monitor Genie Agents usage with audit logs and alerts .\nL20:   * Catch failures in agents: Alert on agent quality metrics so failures and emerging issues surface during development and operation. See cite12†Set up production monitoring .\nL21:   * Run an alert as a task in Lakeflow Jobs: Add an alert as a task so condition checks run on a pipeline trigger and downstream tasks can branch on the result. See cite13†SQL alert task for jobs .\nL22: ## Get started with alertscite14†​ L23: \nL24: The following pages cover the most common alert tasks, from authoring a new alert to ongoing management:\nL25:   *     * cite15†Create an alert L26:     * Walk through the alert editor end-to-end. Includes advanced settings and notification template customization.\nL27:   *     * cite16†Manage alerts L28:     * Find alerts on the listing page, share them, transfer ownership, and track changes with Databricks Git folders.\nL29:   *     * cite13†Run alerts as tasks in Lakeflow Jobs L30:     * Add a Databricks SQL alert as a task in a job so condition checks run on a pipeline trigger and downstream tasks can branch on the result.\nL31:   *     * cite17†Alert query patterns L32:     * SQL patterns for aggregations, multicolumn conditions, and metric views.\nL33: ## Differences from legacy alertscite18†​ L34: \nL35: The latest version of Databricks SQL alerts behaves differently from legacy alerts in a few key ways:\nL36: \nL37:   * Query reuse: An existing saved SQL query cannot be reused when creating an alert. Each alert owns its query definition, which can be authored directly in the new alert editor.\nL38:   * Alert status values: Alert states are simplified and alerts no longer support the `UNKNOWN` status from legacy alerts. Evaluations resolve to `OK`, `TRIGGERED`, or `ERROR`.\nL39: You can continue to use both the latest alerts and legacy alerts side by side while you transition.\nL40: \nL41: On this page\nL42: \nL43:   * cite3†What you can do with alerts L44:   * cite14†Get started with alerts L45:   * cite18†Differences from legacy alerts L46: \nL47: cite19†Privacy Notice†www.databricks.com ·cite20†Terms of Use†www.databricks.com ·cite21†Modern Slavery Statement†www.databricks.com ·cite22†California Privacy†www.databricks.com ·Your Privacy Choices cite23†Image†www.databricks.com "
